"Ars Combinatoria Archives - Combinatorial Press (https://combinatorialpress.com/ars-articles/)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; The stretched Littlewood-Richardson coefficient \\(c^{t\\nu}_{t\\lambda,t\\mu}\\) was conjectured by King, Tollu, and Toumazet to be a polynomial function in \\(t\\).\n\n  * Research article\n  * https://www.doi.org/10.61091/ars162-15\n  * Full Text\n\n  * Download PDF\n\n#### A short proof for the polynomiality of the stretched littlewood-Richardson coefficients\n\n^{^{1}}^{}^{}\n\n^{1}Department of Mathematics, University of California, Davis, California USA\n\n  * Ars Combinatoria[Input]\n  * Volume 162\n  * Pages: 205-212\n  * Published Online: 29/03/2025\n\nAbstract:\n\nThe stretched Littlewood-Richardson coefficient \\(c^{t\\nu}_{t\\lambda,t\\mu}\\) was conjectured by King, Tollu, and Toumazet to be a polynomial function in \\(t\\). It was shown to be true by Derksen and Weyman using semi-invariants of quivers. Later, Rassart used Steinberg’s formula, the hive conditions, and the Kostant partition function to show a stronger result that \\(c^{\\nu}_{\\lambda,\\mu}\\) is indeed a polynomial in variables \\(\\nu, \\lambda, \\mu\\) provided they lie in certain polyhedral cones. Motivated by Rassart’s approach, we give a short alternative proof of the polynomiality of \\(c^{t\\nu}_{t\\lambda,t\\mu}\\) using Steinberg’s formula and a simple argument about the chamber complex of the Kostant partition function.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTHE HIVE MODEL AND THE POLYNOMIAL NATURE OF STRETCHED LITTLEWOOD{RICHARDSON COEFFICIENTS (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/251814043_THE_HIVE_MODEL_AND_THE_POLYNOMIAL_NATURE_OF_STRETCHED_LITTLEWOODRICHARDSON_COEFFICIENTS)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 21.9 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; complished, under the hypotheses of the Conjecture, by a generalisation of the arguments ... a knowledge of the degree of the polynomial would be extremely advantageous. ... STRETCHED LITTLEWOOD–RICHARDSON COEFFICIENTS 13\n\nmay factorise. Although rather easy to prove using semistandard tableaux, this factori-\n\nsation property may also be established through the use of K-hives [KTT2]. Analogous\n\nmethods may be used to show that some Littlewood–Richardson coeﬃcients may also fac-\n\ntorise. Following the use of similar terminology in the case of Kostka coeﬃcients [KTT2],\n\nwe propose the following\n\nDeﬁnition 4.1. Let λ, µ and ν be partitions such that |ν| = |λ| + |µ|, `(λ), `(µ), `(ν) ≤ n\n\nand c\n\nν\n\nλµ\n\n> 0. Then the Littlewood–Richardson coeﬃcient c\n\nν\n\nλµ\n\nis said to be primitive if\n\nps (ν)\n\nK\n\n< ps (λ)\n\nI\n\n+ ps (µ)\n\nJ\n\nfor all (I, J, K) ∈ R\n\nn\n\nr\n\nand all r = 1, 2, . . . , n − 1. Conversely,\n\nc\n\nν\n\nλµ\n\nis not primitive if there exists any (I, J, K) ∈ R\n\nn\n\nr\n\nwith 1 ≤ r < n such that ps (ν)\n\nK\n\n=\n\nps (λ)\n\nI\n\n+ ps (µ)\n\nJ\n\n.\n\nWith this deﬁnition, we conjecture that if c\n\nν\n\nλµ\n\nis positive but not primitive, then c\n\nν\n\nλµ\n\nfactorises. In order to specify the manner in which such a non-primitive Littlewood–\n\nRichardson coeﬃcient factorises it is convenient to introduce some further notation. As\n\nusual, let n be a ﬁxed positive integer and let N = {1, 2, . . . , n}. Then for any I =\n\n{i\n\n1\n\n, i\n\n2\n\n, . . . , i\n\nr\n\n} ⊆ N, with i\n\n1\n\n< i\n\n2\n\n< · · · < i\n\nr\n\nand 1 ≤ r ≤ n, let\n\nI = N\\I be the\n\ncomplement of I in N. In addition, for any partition or weight κ = (κ\n\n1\n\n, κ\n\n2\n\n, . . . , κ\n\nn\n\n) let\n\nκ\n\nI\n\n= (κ\n\ni\n\n1\n\n, κ\n\ni\n\n2\n\n, . . . , κ\n\ni\n\nr\n\n). With this notation we make the following:\n\nConjecture 4.2. Let λ, µ and ν be partitions of lengths `(λ), `(µ), `(ν) ≤ n. If c\n\nν\n\nλµ\n\n> 0\n\nand there exists proper subsets I, J, K of N with (I, J, K) ∈ R\n\nn\n\nr\n\nsuch that ps (ν)\n\nK\n\n=\n\nps (λ)\n\nI\n\n+ ps (µ)\n\nJ\n\nthen\n\n(4.1) c\n\nν\n\nλµ\n\n= c\n\nν\n\nK\n\nλ\n\nI\n\nµ\n\nJ\n\nc\n\nν\n\nK\n\nλ\n\n.\n\nThe redundancy of the corridors is rather easily established by showing that the corre-\n\nsponding Horn equality is suﬃcient to ensure that all their interior edge labels are ﬁxed\n\nby those on their boundary. It is then not diﬃcult to show that the LR hive conditions for\n\nH\n\nn\n\nimply the validity of the LR hive conditions for any rhombus of either H\n\nr\n\nor H\n\nn−r\n\nthat\n\nis split by a corridor in H\n\nn\n\n. To complete the proof it is necessary to show, conversely, that\n\nthe LR hive conditions of H\n\nr\n\nand H\n\nn−r\n\nare suﬃcient to imply the LR hive conditions of\n\nH\n\nn\n\nfor all rhombi that are split by a redundant corridor. It is believed that this may be ac-\n\ncomplished, under the hypotheses of the Conjecture, by a generalisation of the arguments\n\nused elsewhere [KTT2] in proving factorisation of some Kostka coeﬃcients. However, in\n\nthe present context, it should be noted that the required result is not true in the case of all\n\nrhombi of H\n\nn\n\nthat are split by a redundant corridor arising from a puzzle associated with\n\nthe saturation of an inessential Horn inequality. This is connected with the fact that in such\n\ncases the puzzle itself is not unique [KTW]. It is for this reason that the Conjecture 4.2\n\napplies only to the saturation of an essential Horn inequality, for which the correspond-\n\ning puzzle is known to be unique [KTW], and the corresponding Littlewood–Richardson\n\ncoeﬃcient is not primitive.\n\n5. Degrees of stretched polynomials\n\nEven given a certain amount of factorisation that reduces the evaluation of stretched\n\nLittlewood–Richardson polynomials to that of calculating these LR-polynomials in the\n\nprimitive case, their evaluation may be combinatorially formidable. In any given case\n\na knowledge of the degree of the polynomial would be extremely advantageous. Here we\n\nestablish an upper bound on this degree by means of the following rather innocuous looking\n\nobservation.\n\nTaking α = γ in each of the 5-vertex diagrams encountered earlier, gives\n\nα\n\nα\n\nα\n\nβ\n\nβ\n\nα\n\nα\n\nα\n\nβ\n\nIn each case the rhombus constraints give α ≥ β ≥ α so that we must have β = α. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Journal of Combinatorial Theory, Series A, Volume 118 (https://dblp.org/db/journals/jct/jcta118.html)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Generalised stretched Littlewood-Richardson coefficients.\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/jct/Gutschwager11\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n      * CiteSeerX\n      * PubPeer\n\n    * share record\n\n      * Bluesky\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/jct/Gutschwager11\n\nChristian Gutschwager:\nGeneralised stretched Littlewood-Richardson coefficients. 1829-1842\n  * Image: Journal Articles\n\n    * view\n\n      * electronic edition via DOI (open access)\n      * details & citations\n\nauthority control:\n\n      *  \n\n    * export record\n\n      * BibTeX\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ns63preface (https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~slc/wpapers/s63preface.html)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.1 years ago; Crawled: 17.1 years ago;   * Christian Gutschwager (Leibniz Universität Hannover): Generalized Stretched Littlewood-Richardson Coefficients\n  * Jacques Désarménien (Université Marne-la-Vallée)\n  * Gérard Duchamp (Université Paris-Nord)\n  * Theresia Eisenkölbl (Université Lyon 1)\n  * Luca Ferrari (Università di Firenze)\n  * Roberta Fulci (Università di Bologna)\n  * Henri Gaudier (Strasbourg)\n  * Yoann Gelineau (Université Lyon 1): Combinatorial interpretations of the Jacobi-Stirling numbers\n  * Antonio Giambruno (Un